{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"emotion as a design value emotion as a design value","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md","fileName":"Emotion as a Design Value.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Emotion as a Design Value","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 00:27\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Design Values]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Design Values","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"emotion as a design value emotion as a design value","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md","fileName":"Emotion as a Design Value.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Emotion as a Design Value","heading":"Emotion as a Design Value","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An emotion design value names the feelings a game should make possible and connects them to the play that may produce them. Tension, tenderness, embarrassment, triumph, uncertainty, grief, or delight require different patterns of action, consequence, timing, and presentation.\n\nThe value is an intention rather than a guaranteed output because players bring different histories and interpretations. Playtesting asks whether the designed conditions repeatedly support the emotional direction, not whether every person reports an identical feeling.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Design Values","gamesdesignandplay.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design Value.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Emotion as a Design V